`count` writes memory its caller can see at tests/wordings/nl2471_mutex_task_unguarded.ts:23:3, and `spawn` runs it beside the scope's other tasks: a task may write shared memory only through the guard of a `Mutex`, `using g = m.lock()`. Scratch only this task uses is made inside it, `const t = new T()`: an object handed in as the argument is shared memory even when the `spawn` made it, because where an argument was made is not tracked. A value the parent needs back is the task's answer, `s.spawn(task, arg, out, i)`
